            {
              "id": "java/HttpResponseSplitting",
              "name": "HttpResponseSplitting",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Improper Neutralization of CRLF Sequences in HTTP Headers"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "\nIf unvalidated data reaches an HTTP header, this may allow an attacker to specify the entirety of an HTTP response to be rendered by the browser. If an HTTP request contains unexpected CR (carriage return or \\r) and LF (line feed or \\n) characters the server will respond with an output stream that is interpreted as two different HTTP responses. An attacker can control the second response and may mount a range of attacks such as cross-site scripting, cache poisoning and others.\n\nA solution to this problem is to not allow user-controlled data to directly reach HTTP response headers or to validate the data to never include CR or LF characters.\n\nFor example, the following code is vulnerable:\n```\nprotected void doGet(HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response) {\n  Cookie cookie = new Cookie(\"name\", request.getParameter(\"name\"));\n  response.addCookie(cookie);\n}\n```\n\nbecause the user may provide a name parameter with a value like `XYZ\\r\\nHTTP/1.1 200 OK\\nHACKER CONTROLLED`. In this case, they will produce a second HTTP response:\n\n```\nHTTP/1.1 200 OK\nHACKER CONTROLLED\n```\n\nA possible fix is to remove all non-alphanumerical characters:\n```\nprotected void doGet(HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response) {\n  String name = request.getParameter(\"name\")\n      .replaceAll(\"[^a-zA-Z ]\", \"\");\n  Cookie cookie = new Cookie(\"name\", name);\n  response.addCookie(cookie);\n}\n```\n\nIn this case, the attacker would be unable to produce a second HTTP response.",
                "text": ""
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "java",
                  "maintenance",
                  "cookie",
                  "servlet",
                  "request"
                ],
                "categories": [
                  "Security"
                ],
